From: schooley@new-orleans.neosoft.com (John Schooley)
Date: Fri, 19 Aug 1994 15:07:24 -0600
Subject: [#94-8-222] Does Existence Exist?

>>Reilly Jones  writes:
>A fundamental question one can ask is:  What things in what universes
>subjectively experience what?>
>
>A term I have seen used by researchers doing this kind of modelling, is
>"artificial realities."  The limited attempts to answer the fundamental
>question
>posed above, that I have seen so far, have been within a context of
>functionally
>integrated interactions by self-contained entities with their environment and
>other self-contained entities.  If the self-contained entities are
>programmable,
>I would question whether they are capable of subjective experience because they
>are not unique, they are functionally deterministic.  But, I have never seen a
>good definition of "programmable" or "program" so I don't know for certain what
>I am saying here.  I would like to see a good definition someday, so that I
>might begin to figure out what I want to think about this important topic of
>subjective artificial realities.  My guess now, in the absence of good
>definitions, is that all experience in artificial realities is objective, and
>that only within real existence (i.e., not simulated, modelled or hypothesized)
>do we find subjectivity.  I am certain that I am uncertain about this.
>

It is unlikely that your self contained entities of any complexity are
programmable and therefore deterministic in the sense you seem to mean.  
They would fall under the category of highly complex and by nature
indeterministic (if you mean determinism in a strong sense) systems such as
the weather, or you or I.  You can easily say that our genetic code is a
program that plays us but the determinism breaks down when we try to pin it
down, even theoretically.  I have no problem with self-contained entities
within a "universe" being capable of subjectivity, at least that's my
subjective belief.

