From: Eric Watt Forste <arkuat@netcom.com>
Date: Fri, 19 Aug 94 16:32:33 -0700
Subject: [#94-8-224] EPIST: Is Certainty Relevant? 

>Yes, it's a conspiracy plot by all the dictionary makers to make us
>think that "certain" means "free from doubt, indubitable."

Dictionaries report usage. That the common usage of a word makes an
implicit claim is not sufficient evidence for the *truth* of that
implicit claim.

Common usage of words once made it self-evident that the celestial
sphere and all the stars in the sky revolved about the earth. Common
usage of words still makes it clear that heat is fluid substance, even
though nowadays we know this to be false. If you want to make an argument,
you'll have to do better than this.

>Eric: <"Certainty is relative" simply means that you are more certain
>of some certain things than you are of other certain things.>
>
>Groan!  Then you have become uncertain of at least some of them.

So, to bring a question which you never answered out of the Wayback
Machine, are you going to claim that you are exactly *as* certain that
Deng Xiaoping exists *as* you are certain that you have a nose
attached to your face? You claim that the degree of your certainty in
these two beliefs is *identical*? Or rather, do you claim to be
uncertain about the existence of Deng Xiaoping? You can't have it both
ways, Reilly. Choose and tell.

