From: sw@tiac.net (Steve Witham)
Date: Fri, 19 Aug 1994 22:51:55 -0400
Subject: [#94-8-227] Does existence exist?

Charles says-

>You're flying a plane.  It's pitch dark outside.  The mountainside
>you're about to fly into is completely invisible, so therefore it
>doesn't exist??????   Can you apply this theory after you have
>crashed into the mountain.  I will gamble that the tried and true
>indicators of the physical universe (at least in this dimension)
>have clearly demonstrated their existance.

The straight answer is that crashing into a mountain demonstrates the
existence of the mountain within the universe, not that the universe was also
existing within some other universe or simulation.

But I think maybe Charles meant to draw an analogy.  If the existence of the
universe isn't visible to the inhabitants, does that mean the universe doesn't
exist?????  No, neither that nor the opposite.  Not knowing about something
doesn't make it false *or* true.  But something you're *never* going to run
into is irrelevant.

Mike Price says-

>I think the existence [*] of some implementation is sufficient to ensure
>that the experiences exist and are and similar, no matter how different
>the implementation is.
>...
>[*] I haven't defined "exist" and "realised", because I don't know what
>it means, so I haven't answered the original question posed.

I think that the existence of an implementation is also irrelevant.
But I agree that worlds or chunks of worlds that map to each other have the
"same" experiences.  (For instance, the same program in the same sequence
of states on two different simulators.  Or compiled for two different
processors.  Or the equivalent program written in two different languages.
Or implemented using two different programming methods, but giving
equivalent results.  Or...never run, never compiled, never written, or never
contemplated.)

I'm not sure a state without rules for "succeeding" states (or something like
laws of physics relating states in neighboring times and places) has any
experience.  One interesting point I think Hawking made is that if you don't
have entropy, you don't have anything like memory.

Btw, there's an interesting micro debate about the Many-Worlds hypothesis in
the letters column of the current Scientific American, something like:
     A:  Many Worlds violates Occam's razor because of all those extra worlds!
     B:  Occam's razor properly means minimizing concepts, not instances,
         and Many Worlds has fewer concepts than the Copenhagen interpretation.

But there's still that irritating question: if all possible worlds have
equal (or at least indistinguishable) existence, then what is probability?

[Much good stuff of Mike's deleted, including his response to Robin asking
 "when" an experience happened, e.g., if you generated a lookup table before
 and used to simulate something now.]

If Mickey Mouse looks at his watch and it says 5:30, then *that's* when he
experiences it.  When you simulate it is like when you go see a movie--
irrelevant to the plot.

 --Steve
