From: sw@tiac.net (Steve Witham)
Date: Fri, 19 Aug 1994 23:40:02 -0400
Subject: [#94-8-229] Does existence exist?

Picking up Robin in the middle of a thread...

>But the novel feature of this scenario is the use of *interpolation*.
>None of the interpolated situations happened before during the prior
>simulations, though *very* similar situations did.  Are there new
>interpolated experiences cooresponding to the interpolated I/O
>patterns generated?

I think a chunk of history that's simply an interpolation between two
others doesn't have an experience of its own because its evolution doesn't
follow from its own state.  You might say any set of self-determined
universes taken together could be called a universe...but I don't think
that's right either, since they don't affect each other.  A universe ought
to be unitary--a transitive closure of interacting stuff.

Robin-
>>> What if an improbable thermal fluctuation created the same pattern,
>>> now across just space instead of space-time, but without the same
>>> causal structure?  Would that pattern experience?

Mike Price-
>>No, because there was/is/will be no implementation.

Robin-
>You must mean something special by "implementation".  In a literal
>sense, the pattern happened, and therefore it had some implementation.

I think if the same laws of physics applied once the state came together,
then it would have the same experiences, but if not, not.  Not all states
can be arrived at by more than one path in all laws of physics, though.
If you break the laws, you're making a more complicated set of laws.

(Which brings up communication between the inside and outside of a simulator.
That also changes the rules.)

>But there is the common, and it seems to me self-consistent, view that
>it just isn't like anything inside inside a universe that doesn't exist.

I think it's maybe self-consistent, but adds nothing.  That is, it doesn't
help explain or find out about anything to say that a universe does or
doesn't exist.  If I have a way of looking at a story from within to see
what it would have been like for one of the characters, what difference
does it make that the story "never happened?"  What sense does it make, or
what good does it do, to say that it "just isn't like anything" to be that
character, if I can imagine what it's like, and the only difference is that
the story didn't happen?

I wonder how many people have really examined these points and are still
firm on that difference.  It seems like a distinction looking for a meaning,
like the "zombie" vs. "real, experiencing person" distinction, which Dennett
says real philosophers ponder.

>>So Minsky drops a lot of baggage at the door, but you go in.

He just said that existence of the universe is irrelevant to experience
within it, while you started asking detailed questions about experience.
But maybe you're still left holding the bag!-)

Ooh, good metaphor!  Philosophical baggage = distinctions *without content*.
Holding the bag = pondering a distinction with no content.

 --Steve
